Congrats on the Gemma 4 launch from @googlegemma; day-0 support is now live in SGLang! Gemma 4 is a multimodal family with four sizes (E2B, E4B, 26B A4B, and 31B) featuring both Dense and MoE architectures, designed for everything from mobile to server-scale.

It offers rich multimodal understanding across text, image, video, and audio in one model, a built-in thinking mode for configurable step-by-step reasoning, massive context support with up to 256K tokens for medium models, and native function calling for agentic workflows.

@googlegemma: "Meet Gemma 4! Purpose-built for advanced reasoning and agentic workflows on the hardware you own, and released under an Apache 2.0 license. We listened to invaluable community feedback in developing these models. Here is what makes Gemma 4 our most capable open models yet."

We built a course with @DeepLearningAI on how to run LLM and image generation faster and more efficiently. LLM inference has a redundancy problem, as your chatbot re-reads the same system prompt on every request, and your coding agent re-processes the full context before every tool call. This redundancy can be overwhelming across billions of requests from millions of users.

SGLang was built to solve this with RadixAttention, ensuring every response is unique while minimizing redundant work. The course, lasting just over an hour, covers how inference works with SGLang, caching with diffusion models, and the future of AI inference. The most advanced infrastructure for serving modern AI is now open and easier than ever to learn.

@DeepLearningAI: "New course available! Efficient Inference with SGLang: Text and Image Generation is live. LLM inference gets expensive fast—mostly due to redundant computation. This course shows how to reduce that using SGLang, with KV cache and RadixAttention, and how to apply the same ideas to faster image generation."

Congrats on releasing MiniMax M2.7 from @MiniMax_AI, the first model deeply participating in its own evolution. Day-0 support is now live in SGLang, with the model autonomously optimizing a scaffold over 100+ rounds, achieving a 30% performance gain.

It boasts a 66.6% medal rate on MLE Bench Lite, ranking #2 overall behind Opus 4.6 and GPT-5.4. SWE performance includes SWE-Pro at 56.22%, SWE Multilingual at 76.5%, and Multi SWE Bench at 52.7%. Additionally, it has an ELO of 1495 on GDPval-AA, the highest for open-source models, and a score of 62.7% on MM Claw. Native Agent Teams enable stable multi-agent collaboration, optimized by @nvidia with SGLang. You can try it on NVIDIA NIM Endpoint.

@MiniMax_AI: "We're delighted to announce that MiniMax M2.7 is now officially open source. With SOTA performance in SWE-Pro (56.22%) and Terminal Bench 2 (57.0%). You can find it on Hugging Face now. Enjoy!"

Meet Hy3 preview from @TencentHunyuan, a 295B hybrid mixture of experts (MoE) model with 21B active parameters and 256K context. Day-0 support is now live in SGLang, showcasing strong reasoning capabilities with top results on FrontierScience-Olympiad, IMO-AnswerBench, and GPQA Diamond.

The model achieves significant gains on CL-bench and LongBench v2 due to its 256K context. It is competitive on SWE-Bench Verified, Terminal-Bench 2.0, and BrowseComp for code and agent tasks. Co-designed with Yuanbao, Hy3 aims for sharper intent and natural replies.

@TencentHunyuan: "Hi, we're the Tencent Hy team. Today, we open source Hy3 preview (295B A21B), a leading reasoning and agent model in its size, with great cost efficiency. Give us feedback to help improve Hy3 official!"

SGLang is now powering our close partner @modal for Deepseek V4 inference with 250 tokens per second in real-world coding workloads. This milestone reflects significant new work across the SGLang serving stack, including techniques like ShadowRadix and HiSparse.

@charles_irl: "Try it today on @modal! For coding tasks with high specdec, I saw up to 250 decode tokens per second from @sgl_project for a single user of V4 Pro on an 8xB200."

The linked documentation explains that the DeepSeek V4 Pro weights are delivered in mixed MXFP4 and run through SGLang’s flashinfer_mxfp4 MoE runner backend on Blackwell, utilizing EAGLE speculative decoding for low to moderate concurrency time per output token.

Day 0 support on both GPU and TPU this time! 

@lmsysorg: "🎉 MiMo-V2.5 series is here, day-0 support is now live in SGLang! Two models to try: 1️⃣ MiMo-V2.5-Pro: 1.02T/42B MoE, hybrid attention, up to 1M context; 2️⃣ MiMo-V2.5: full multimodal (text, image, video, audio), 310B/15B MoE, 1M context. We also have day 0 support for this model in sglang-jax, check for details. Cookbook: Run it now with SGLang!" 

The MiMo-V2.5 series includes advanced models with significant context capabilities, supporting multiple data types.

Great to see this from @NVIDIAAI. SGLang has become a strong production choice for DeepSeek V4 serving due to native ShadowRadix prefix caching, HiSparse KV hierarchy, context parallelism, stable compression kernels, and Day-0 MTP/MegaMoE support. These optimizations are especially important for agentic workloads; even at ~1M context, SGLang only sees ~10% degradation compared with short input lengths.

@NVIDIAAI: "SGLang is hitting 180 tok/s/GPU on DeepSeek-V4 decode with ~1M context on Blackwell. Good to see fast progress in open source DeepSeek-V4 inference on new hardware. This comes from Blackwell-specific optimizations by @lmsysorg that better use the model’s hybrid sparse attention."

The linked blog discusses Day-0 support for DeepSeek-V4 across both inference and reinforcement learning training, highlighting SGLang and Miles as the first open-source stack to serve and train DeepSeek-V4 on launch day.

We at Accel are excited to be leading RadixArk’s $100M seed round alongside our friends at Spark Capital. It’s been a privilege to partner with Ying and Banghua since inception and to support the incredible LMSYS community.

Intelligence is no longer the bottleneck; developers are now constrained by their ability to control, adapt, and reliably serve a growing diversity of AI models across hardware, environments, and use cases at scale. This shift creates an opportunity to build new foundational infrastructure for training and inference. RadixArk’s mission is to build that open infrastructure, and we’re excited to be their partners on the journey.

@radixark: "Today, we are thrilled to officially launch RadixArk with $100M in Seed funding at a $400M valuation. The round was led by Accel and co-led by Spark Capital. RadixArk exists to make frontier AI infrastructure open and accessible to everyone. Today, the systems behind the most capable AI models are concentrated in a small number of companies. As a result, most AI teams are forced to rebuild training and inference stacks from scratch, duplicating the same infrastructure work instead of focusing on new models, products, and ideas. RadixArk was founded to change that. We are building an AI platform that makes it easier for teams to train and serve the best models at scale. RadixArk comes from the open-source community. We started with SGLang, where many of us are core developers and maintainers, and expanded our work to Miles for large-scale RL and post-training. We will continue contributing to both projects and working with the community to make them the strongest open-source infrastructure foundations for frontier AI."

